                <h2>This Week's Observation: Reconciliation Anxiety Peaks</h2>
<p>As year-end approaches, overseas ad platforms are closing their monthly billing cycles. Reports from Meta, Google, TikTok, Pinterest and other major channels are being released intensively, and reconciliation needs from advertisers and agencies are surging.</p>
<p>This week, consultations related to reconciliation received through Yiyu Media's support channels increased by approximately 40% compared to the same period last month. Issues cluster around three dimensions: exchange rate gaps between top-ups and credited amounts, discrepancies between platform spend reports and financial records, and misaligned invoice information with报销 requirements. These three "potholes" may seem basic, yet they frequently appear in real operations of multi-account, multi-platform teams.</p>
<p>This week's observation isn't intended as a textbook-style reconciliation guide. Instead, we analyze the causes and solutions based on real consultation cases from this week.</p>
<hr />
<h2>The List: Three High-Frequency Reconciliation Issues This Week</h2>
<h3>1. Exchange Rate Gaps — The credited amount differs from what you expected</h3>
<p><strong>Symptom</strong>: A cross-border e-commerce team concentrated their Google Ads top-ups early in the month using a locked-in favorable exchange rate. However, the platform's actual credited rate was calculated at the real-time rate on the top-up day, resulting in a final credited amount approximately 1.2% lower than expected. Finance discovered a gap between account balance and total top-up at month-end.</p>
<p><strong>Root Cause</strong>: <br />
- Time lag between platform top-up rates and contractually agreed rates<br />
- Some platforms offer exchange rate protection for large top-ups, but pre-application is required<br />
- Month-end top-ups face greater exchange rate volatility and risk exposure</p>
<p><strong>Editor's Recommendation</strong>: Build a "top-up calendar" and plan large top-up timing windows in advance to avoid concentrated top-ups during rate peaks.</p>
<h3>2. Spend Data Delays — Numbers look different today vs. tomorrow</h3>
<p><strong>Symptom</strong>: A utility app's advertising team checked Meta's backend on November 30th. The spend report showed total monthly spend at $128,000. When checked again on December 1st, the same period's data updated to $131,000. Finance found discrepancies when reconciling.</p>
<p><strong>Root Cause</strong>: <br />
- Overseas platforms have 24-72 hour data sync delays<br />
- Timezone setting differences cause spend attribution date misalignments<br />
- Cross-day timezones (especially US Eastern/UTC) spend attribution are problem hotspots</p>
<p><strong>Editor's Recommendation</strong>: Use the platform's final settlement report as the reconciliation basis, not the real-time dashboard. It's recommended to confirm the previous month's spend after the 5th of each month.</p>
<h3>3. Invoice Information Mismatches — Payment made, but invoice can't be issued</h3>
<p><strong>Symptom</strong>: An overseas gaming company uses multiple entities to contract with Google, Meta, and TikTok respectively. During finance reconciliation, they found that some invoice tax numbers and addresses had minor discrepancies with company registration information, causing reimbursement processes to stall.</p>
<p><strong>Root Cause</strong>: <br />
- Account opening entities don't match receiving entities<br />
- Invoice templates weren't confirmed with finance in advance<br />
- Platform invoice issuance cycles are long (typically 5-15 business days)</p>
<p><strong>Editor's Recommendation</strong>: Confirm invoice template requirements with finance during the account setup phase, ensuring consistent invoice recipient information across all platform accounts.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Case Study: How Multi-Account Teams Perform Three-Way Matching</h2>
<p>A multi-platform advertising team served by Yiyu Media uses the following process for monthly three-way matching:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Export top-up records</strong>: Download top-up details from each platform backend, including top-up time, amount, and exchange rate</li>
<li><strong>Download spend reports</strong>: Wait for the platform's settlement cycle to complete (typically after the 3rd-5th of each month) before downloading official reports</li>
<li><strong>Invoice verification</strong>: Check whether invoice amounts match top-up amounts; note the distinction between "prepaid top-up" and "spend deduction" scenarios</li>
<li><strong>Variance annotation</strong>: Provide written explanations for minor discrepancies caused by exchange rate differences or timezone shifts</li>
<li><strong>Archive for retention</strong>: Keep reconciliation records on file for quarterly audits</li>
</ol>
<p>The team lead reported that after adopting this standardized process, monthly reconciliation time reduced from 3 person-days to 0.5 person-days.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Year-End Reconciliation Checklist — Save for Reference</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Confirm all platform statements are stable (recommended: operate after the 5th of each month)</li>
<li>[ ] Cross-check top-up records with platform credited transaction details</li>
<li>[ ] Verify timezone settings to ensure correct spend attribution</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm invoice recipient names, tax numbers, and addresses match company information</li>
<li>[ ] Record exchange rate variances and prepare finance explanation documentation</li>
<li>[ ] Multi-account teams should consider unified reconciliation tools to avoid manual Excel consolidation</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Editor's Note: No Preparation Now Means Tears at Year-End</h2>
<p>Overseas ad reconciliation isn't something to worry about only at year-end. By maintaining proper top-up records, spend verification, and invoice archiving in your daily operations, you can handle year-end reconciliation season with ease.</p>
<p>For overseas teams managing multiple platforms and accounts, establishing a standardized reconciliation SOP as early as possible is strongly advised. If you don't currently have a systematic process in place, now is the perfect time to build one — don't wait until the evening of December 31st only to discover your accounts don't match.</p>
